   Applications, Valencia, SPAIN, SEP 17-20, 2024},
Abstract = {Artificial intelligence has sparked significant business revolutions.
   Various benefits have recently been identified in the analysis of health
   data, encompassing applications such as diagnostics utilizing personal
   monitoring devices, analysis of electronic medical images,
   interpretation of laboratory test reports, and assistance in formulating
   health policies, all of which are pertinent to the healthcare system.
   However, the enactment of data protection laws in Brazil (LGPD) and the
   European Union (GDPR) has posed challenges in accessing patients'
   personal health data, consequently impeding progress in the field. The
   European Health Data Space (EHDS) initiative represents a novel standard
   for sharing health data for research purposes. One proposed solution to
   address this challenge is the Federated Learning (FL) architecture, a
   technique whereby algorithms process data locally and optimize training
   globally without transmitting sensitive data to external entities.
   Numerous open-source tools are available for deploying an FL
   environment, each characterized by distinct architectures,
   functionalities and cryptographic mechanisms. This article scrutinizes
   several open-source FL tools, comparing their functionalities in order
   to ensure compliance with privacy laws for integration into the EHDS
   framework. Finally, we provide concluding remarks and suggest potential
   avenues for future research.},
